      "side": 1,
      "message": "I disagree. There needs to be a way to enable/disable body detection easily from the EC for testing that the various DPTC tables are applied correctly, wake events are not generated, etc.\n\nUsing these `motionsense` commands is too esoteric. Nothing in this output indicates what the magic command `4 1` means:\n\n```\nlocalhost /var/log # ectool motionsense set_activities\nUsage:\n  motionsense                              - dump all motion data\n  motionsense active                       - print active flag\n  motionsense info NUM                     - print sensor info\n  motionsense ec_rate NUM [RATE_MS]        - set/get sample rate\n  motionsense odr NUM [ODR [ROUNDUP]]      - set/get sensor ODR\n  motionsense range NUM [RANGE [ROUNDUP]]  - set/get sensor range\n  motionsense offset NUM [-- X Y Z [TEMP]] - set/get sensor offset\n  motionsense kb_wake NUM                  - set/get KB wake ang\n  motionsense fifo_info                    - print fifo info\n  motionsense fifo_int_enable [0/1]        - enable/disable/get fifo interrupt status\n  motionsense fifo_read MAX_DATA           - read fifo data\n  motionsense fifo_flush NUM               - trigger fifo interrupt\n  motionsense list_activities              - list supported activities\n  motionsense set_activity ACT EN          - enable/disable activity\n  motionsense get_activity ACT             - get activity status\n  motionsense lid_angle                    - print lid angle\n  motionsense spoof -- NUM [0/1] [X Y Z]   - enable/disable spoofing\n  motionsense spoof -- NUM activity ACT [0/1] [STATE] - enable/disable activity spoofing\n  motionsense tablet_mode_angle ANG HYS    - set/get tablet mode angle\n  motionsense calibrate NUM                - run sensor calibration\n```\n\nAdditionally, using `ectool` is impossible when the AP is suspended, meaning host events can be debugged in that situation, since there isn\u0027t a corresponding command in the EC CLI.",
